ANCHOR is not evaluated on public research infrastructure (CloudLab, Chameleon,
SPHERE, FABRIC, Google Colab) for the paper's reference configuration, for one
reason: the reported measurements were produced with a locally served
open-weights model, Qwen/Qwen3.5-35B-A3B-FP8, on an ASUS Ascent GX10 built
around the NVIDIA GB10 Grace Blackwell Superchip with 128 GB of coherent
unified memory. Paper Challenge 3 is specifically about privacy-preserving CTI
analysis with a LOCAL LLM, so the reference configuration is deliberately a
self-hosted endpoint rather than a hosted API. Model weights, GPU hardware, and
a running vLLM service are not redistributable as part of this artifact.

This constraint does NOT block artifact evaluation. ANCHOR resolves its chat
model at runtime from LLM_PROVIDER (artifact/src/utils/llm.py), and all three
providers drive the identical pipeline:

  1. LLM_PROVIDER=openai     A hosted OpenAI-compatible API. No GPU required.
  2. LLM_PROVIDER=anthropic  The Anthropic Messages API. No GPU required.
  3. LLM_PROVIDER=vllm       Any OpenAI-compatible server, including a small
                             model on a modest GPU, a Colab-hosted vLLM, or the
                             authors' reference deployment.

Options 1 and 2 run entirely from a commodity client with no accelerator, so a
reviewer with no GPU allocation can still execute every claim in claims/. The
claims in this package assert that the pipeline runs end to end and emits a
structurally valid ontology-aligned graph; they do not assert the numerical
values in the paper's evaluation tables, and the Results Reproduced badge is not
requested. Substituting a model therefore does not invalidate any claim made
here: it changes the extracted content, not the structural validity that
claims/claim1 and claims/claim2 verify.

claims/claim3 is fully deterministic and needs no endpoint of any kind.

One requirement does carry over to whichever endpoint is used: the model must
support multi-step tool calling. ANCHOR's hybrid ontology discovery is an LLM
agent driving the ontology MCP server through dependent tool calls, so a model
weak at tool calling completes a run and emits a poor or empty graph regardless
of the code. That is a property of the chosen model rather than a defect in the
artifact; artifact/scripts/check_endpoint.py probes it before every claim run and
warns when the model fails, and claim3 isolates package integrity from model
capability.

Hardware detail, including the distinction between the 128 GB reference
configuration and the 64 GB minimum for self-hosting the reference model, is in
HARDWARE_REQUIREMENTS.md. Reference server setup is in VLLM_SETUP.md.
